const favorableLaws = "Idaho is extremely favorable for dynasty trusts because it has completely abolished the rule against perpetuities. Idaho Code § 55-111 provides: 'There shall be no rule against perpetuities applicable to real or personal property.' This allows trusts of unlimited duration — effectively perpetual trusts. Idaho has no state estate tax and no state inheritance tax. Idaho does not currently authorize self-settled domestic asset protection trusts (DAPTs), but the abolition of perpetuities makes it ideal for multi-generational wealth transfer. Idaho also adopted the Uniform Trustees' Powers Act giving trustees broad default powers.";
